papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-179","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Metals typically have lower ionisation enthalpies than the non-metals of the","options":["same group only","same period","f-block exclusively","gaseous state only"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Left-hand (metallic) elements are larger and have smaller Zeff, so they lose electrons more readily.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-180","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Inner-electron screening reduces the effective nuclear charge and thereby","options":["raises ionisation enthalpy","lowers ionisation enthalpy","creates a new period","removes the nucleus"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"A well-shielded valence electron feels less pull and is easier to remove.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-181","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A smaller atomic radius generally corresponds to a","options":["lower ionisation enthalpy","higher ionisation enthalpy","zero ionisation enthalpy","negative electron count"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The outer electron is closer to the nucleus and more tightly bound in a smaller atom.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-182","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Successive ionisation enthalpies of an atom increase because the cation left behind","options":["holds its remaining electrons more tightly","gains a new shell","becomes electrically neutral","has a smaller nuclear charge"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"Each removal raises the charge on the ion and shrinks the cloud, so the next IE is larger.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-183","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The process whose enthalpy change is the first ionisation enthalpy is","options":["$\\mathrm{M}^{+}(g)+e^{-}\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}(g)$","$\\mathrm{M}(g)\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{+}(g)+e^{-}$","$\\mathrm{M}(g)+e^{-}\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}^{-}(g)$","$\\mathrm{M}(s)\\rightarrow \\mathrm{M}(g)$"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"IE₁ is defined for the isolated gaseous atom losing one electron.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-184","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Among the period-2 elements, the highest first ionisation enthalpy belongs to","options":["lithium","fluorine","neon","carbon"],"answer_index":2,"explanation":"Neon is the noble gas of that period and has the most tightly held closed-shell electrons.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-185","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Beryllium has a higher first ionisation enthalpy than boron because Be has a","options":["half-filled 2p set","filled 2s subshell","larger atomic radius than Li","vacant 1s orbital"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Removing an electron from stable 2s² (Be) costs more than removing the single 2p electron of B.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-186","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Nitrogen has a higher first ionisation enthalpy than oxygen because N has a","options":["filled 2s² 2p⁶ shell","half-filled 2p³ set","larger nuclear charge than O","d-orbital vacancy"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The extra stability of half-filled 2p³ outweighs the one-unit rise in Z from N to O.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-187","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Magnesium shows a higher first ionisation enthalpy than aluminium, an exception similar to","options":["Na versus Ar","Be versus B","F versus Ne","K versus Ca"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Mg (3s²) is harder to ionise than Al (3p¹), just as Be (2s²) is harder than B (2p¹).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-188","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Phosphorus has a higher first ionisation enthalpy than sulphur because of the extra stability of the","options":["filled 3s² 3p⁶ set","half-filled 3p³ configuration","empty 3d subshell of S","metallic bond in phosphorus"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"P is 3p³; the paired 3p⁴ electron of S is easier to remove, so IE(P) > IE(S).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-189","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Between lithium and beryllium, beryllium has the greater first ionisation enthalpy because of its","options":["lower Zeff and 2p¹ configuration","higher Zeff and 2s² configuration","much larger radius","noble-gas core of argon"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Be is smaller, has one extra proton and a filled 2s pair, so IE₁(Be) > IE₁(Li).","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-190","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"The second ionisation enthalpy of sodium is very large because the second electron must be taken from the","options":["3s¹ valence orbital","neon-like 2p⁶ core","4s subshell","empty 3d set"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"After loss of 3s¹, Na⁺ is isoelectronic with Ne; breaking that closed shell requires a large IE₂.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-191","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"First ionisation enthalpy falls in the order Li > Na > K because","options":["nuclear charge falls down the group","atomic size increases down the group","the number of valence electrons increases","they become noble gases"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The ns¹ electron is farther from the nucleus and more shielded in Na and K than in Li.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"medium","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-192","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Although oxygen has a higher nuclear charge than nitrogen, its first ionisation enthalpy is lower because","options":["oxygen has the larger atomic radius","the extra electron in 2p⁴ is paired and is easier to remove","nitrogen has a filled 2p⁶ shell","oxygen lacks a 2s subshell"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Size would suggest IE(O) > IE(N), but pairing repulsion in 2p⁴ reverses the trend.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-193","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Boron has a lower first ionisation enthalpy than beryllium even though B is smaller, because","options":["the 2p electron of B is shielded by 2s² and is easier to remove than a 2s electron of Be","boron has a noble-gas configuration","beryllium has a half-filled 2p set","nuclear charge falls from Be to B"],"answer_index":0,"explanation":"The two trends conflict: smaller size would raise IE, but the change of subshell (2s → 2p) lowers it, and the subshell effect wins.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-194","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"For magnesium, the jump between IE₂ and IE₃ is much larger than between IE₁ and IE₂ because the third electron","options":["comes from the 3s subshell","comes from the neon core","is a 3p electron of Al","enters a 4s orbital"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Mg is 3s², so IE₁ and IE₂ remove valence electrons; IE₃ must break the 2p⁶ core and is therefore very large.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"hard","concept_id":"ionization-enthalpy","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-195","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Electron gain enthalpy is the enthalpy change when an electron is added to a neutral gaseous atom to form a","options":["cation","uninegative ion","crystal lattice","hydrated proton"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The defining process is X(g) + e⁻ → X⁻(g); ΔegH is usually negative when energy is released.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-196","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"A more negative electron gain enthalpy means a greater tendency of the atom to","options":["lose a proton","accept an electron","form a cation","expand its nucleus"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The more energy released on adding an electron, the more favourable the gain.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-197","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Halogens have the most negative electron gain enthalpies in their respective periods because they","options":["have empty valence shells","are one electron short of a noble-gas configuration","are the largest atoms of the period","cannot form anions"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Adding one electron completes ns² np⁶ and releases a large amount of energy.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-198","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Noble-gas atoms have positive electron gain enthalpies because the extra electron must","options":["pair in a half-filled p set","enter a new shell","remove a proton","form a metallic bond"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"The incoming electron is added to a higher-n orbital against a closed shell, so energy must be supplied.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-199","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"Electron gain enthalpy generally becomes more negative on moving from left to right in a","options":["group","period","set of isotopes","metallic crystal"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Zeff rises and the vacancy is closer to the nucleus, so electron attachment is more exothermic.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false},{"id":"che-pe-200","chapter_id":"chemistry-classification-of-elements-and-periodicity-in-properties","subject_id":"chemistry","stem":"On moving down a halogen group, electron gain enthalpy generally becomes less negative after","options":["fluorine","chlorine","astatine","helium"],"answer_index":1,"explanation":"Cl has the most negative value; Br and I are less exothermic as the added electron is farther from the nucleus.","images":[],"source":{"name":"ASTRA chemistry practice set","license":"Original syllabus-based practice questions (exam-frequent themes; not verbatim past papers)"},"difficulty":"easy","concept_id":"electron-gain","appear_count":0,"years":[],"exams":[],"verified":false}]$json$::jsonb) as x(
